Summary of Fix for RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED Error
1. Root Cause Analysis
The Firestore error (8 RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED: Quota limit exceeded... for quota metric 'Free daily write units per project (free tier database)') was triggered by:
In server.ts (POST /api/data), a bulk sync handler was iterating through the full dataset (403 members, 25 families, and 124 contributions) and calling setDoc on every document on every sync request (~553 write calls per request).
Combined with client-side synchronization calls on page loads/updates, this rapidly consumed the GCP Free Tier limit of 20,000 writes/day.
When the quota was exceeded, pending unhandled Firestore writes kept triggering the gRPC connection write stream retry loop (0x12278989, 0x1227898a, 0x1227898b).
2. Key Changes Made
Eliminated Bulk Firestore Write Loop in POST /api/data (server.ts):
POST /api/data now synchronizes the server's in-memory currentStore directly without executing loop-based setDoc operations across all collections.
Live Firestore collections (members, contributions, families, settings) remain preserved and untouched.
Added safeFirestoreSetDoc Handler (server.ts):
Individual writes for Telegram messages, receipts, complaints, and member registration now use safeFirestoreSetDoc.
Safely catches code 8 / RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED errors without crashing, re-throwing uncaught exceptions, or triggering infinite stream retry loops.
Added safeClientFirestoreWrite & Quota Fallback (src/firebase.ts):
Wrapped saveMemberToFirestore, saveContributionToFirestore, saveFamilyToFirestore, and saveSettingsToFirestore in safe error handlers.
If the free daily quota limit is reached, operations fail over gracefully to local storage and memory cache without interrupting the user experience or throwing unhandled promise rejections.
Debounced Sync Requests (src/utils/storage.ts):
Debounced syncToBackendServerAsync() to prevent rapid, repetitive calls to /api/data.
Verification:
Both compile_applet and lint_applet passed with zero errors.
Dev server was restarted, verifying that /api/telegram-webhook, GET /api/reports, and POST /api/data all respond with HTTP 200 without emitting quota errors.
